 the argument for on-site capture.</p><h2>Recommendations underwriters act on</h2><p>A good survey recommends risk improvements: fit a fire door, service the sprinklers, clear combustible storage from the boiler room. These are where the surveyor earns the fee, because the underwriter can make them conditions of cover. A vague recommendation is ignored; a specific one with a priority and timescale gets enforced. Structured software prompts for priority and target date every time rather than leaving them to discretion under time pressure.</p><h2>Why capture on site</h2><ul><li><strong>Accuracy:</strong> rate-sensitive facts are recorded in front of the risk, not from memory</li><li><strong>Evidence:</strong> photos of panels, protections and hazards attach to the finding at the finding</li><li><strong>Consistency:</strong> every surveyor produces the same COPE and recommendations structure</li><li><strong>Speed:</strong> the report is largely written by the time the visit ends</li><li><strong>No per-seat penalty:</strong> underwriters and admins who read reports cost nothing to add. Quickler charges per report with unlimited users</li></ul><div class=\"callout\"><strong>Honest note:</strong> software does not make a risk survey sound; the surveyor does. Where the surveyor is RICS-regulated or working to a scheme's requirements, those standards govern the work. Quickler produces clean reports but does not replicate a specific insurer's bespoke form pixel for pixel. This is general reporting software, not underwriting or legal advice. <a href=\"/pages/help/index.html#contact-form-section\">Ask us</a> if a specific format requirement affects your work.</div><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>What is a commercial insurance risk survey report?</h3><p>A commercial insurance risk survey report is a structured document that describes an insured risk so an underwriter can price it and set terms. It covers COPE, the state of fire and security protections, and recommended r